    {
      "id": "2123809824312013221",
      "url": "https://x.com/gaurav_kochar/status/2123809824312013221",
      "author": {
        "name": "Gaurav kochar",
        "username": "gaurav_kochar",
        "avatar_url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1564099511999660033/w_Yhtyf7_normal.jpg",
        "verified": false
      },
      "created_at": "2026-11-20T16:04:55.000Z",
      "text": "INDIA ACHIEVES 20% ETHANOL BLENDING — BUT MAIZE IS EMERGING AS THE BIGGEST WINNER!\n\nIndia has effectively achieved its 20% ethanol blending target in ESY 2025-26, with grain-based feedstocks accounting for a massive 73.52% of total ethanol supplies. This marks a major structural shift in India's ethanol industry, with significant implications for maize, rice and the sugar sector.\n\nKEY NUMBERS — ESY 2025-26:\n\n• Average ethanol blending: 19.99%.\n• Total ethanol blended: 1,134.36 crore litres.\n• Ethanol requirement: 1,050 crore litres.\n• Ethanol allocated: 1,196.90 crore litres.\n• Actual ethanol supplies: 1,088.91 crore litres.\n\nGRAIN vs SUGAR: A MAJOR SHIFT\n\nGrain-based ethanol:\n• Total: 800.53 crore litres.\n• Share: 73.52%.\n\nSugar-based ethanol:\n• Total: 288.38 crore litres.\n• Share: 26.48%.\n\nMAIZE DOMINATES GRAIN-BASED ETHANOL\n\n• Maize: 489.71 crore litres — approximately 45% of total ethanol supplies.\n• Surplus FCI rice: 233.95 crore litres.\n• Damaged foodgrains: 76.87 crore litres.\n\nSUGAR SECTOR CONTRIBUTION\n\n• Sugarcane juice: 153.36 crore litres.\n• B-Heavy molasses: 118.04 crore litres.\n• C-Heavy molasses: 16.98 crore litres.\n\nApproximately 32.45 lakh tonnes of sugar were diverted towards ethanol production:\n• Sugarcane juice: 23 lakh tonnes.\n• B-Heavy molasses: 9.44 lakh tonnes.\n\nTHE BIG AGRICULTURAL MARKET IMPLICATION:\n\nMaize has become the single largest feedstock in India's ethanol programme, supplying nearly 46% of total ethanol supplies according to AIDA.\n\nThis has important consequences:\n\n1. MAIZE: Structural demand from distilleries is strengthening its industrial consumption base and increasing competition between ethanol, poultry and starch industries.\n\n2. RICE: FCI surplus rice continues to provide an alternative feedstock, but maize remains the dominant grain-based source.\n\n3. SUGAR: Sugar diversion of 32.45 lakh tonnes remains significant, although sugar-based ethanol now accounts for only 26.48% of supplies.\n\n4. DISTILLERIES: Feedstock diversification has become central to India's energy security strategy.\n\nMY TAKEAWAY:\n\nIndia's ethanol programme is no longer just a sugar-industry story. It has increasingly become a maize-demand story.\n\nWith grain-based feedstocks supplying nearly three-fourths of ethanol, maize availability, prices and competition from the poultry sector will become increasingly important variables for India's agricultural balance sheet.",
      "lang": "en",
      "public_metrics": {
        "reply_count": 0,
        "repost_count": 0,
        "like_count": 8,
        "quote_count": 0,
        "bookmark_count": 3,
        "impression_count": 610
      },
      "photos": [
        "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HXlJxkkasAAHWn3.jpg"
      ],
      "video_thumbnail": null,
      "has_video": false,
      "title_zh": "印度E20乙醇汽油目标达成：平均掺混19.99%，玉米成最大原料",
      "summary_zh": "帖子援引印度酿酒协会（AIDA）数据称，印度在2025-26乙醇供应年实际完成20%乙醇掺混目标：平均掺混率19.99%，共掺混1134.36克若尔升，需求量为1050克若尔升。粮食类原料占乙醇供应的73.52%，其中玉米489.71克若尔升，约占总供应的45%，成为最大单一原料；糖类原料仅占26.48%，约324.5万吨（原文32.45拉克吨）的糖被转去生产乙醇。作者认为这会加剧乙醇、家禽饲料和淀粉行业对玉米的争夺。",
